From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: maxio: give the A7S's two RJ45 LEDs distinct meanings

Since `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` both LEDs on the Cubie A7S's RJ45 light and blink together with
rx/tx traffic, which is a direct consequence of that patch's own LED fix and
carries no information: two LEDs saying the same thing.

Board wiring first, because the fix is only meaningful against it. The
MAE0621A has three LED pins, of which this board uses two (SCH rev V1.10 p15):

	LED1 / pin 33  -> R93 0R      -> PHY0_G_LED  -> J2  = GREEN
	LED2 / pin 34  -> R101/R103   -> PHY0_Y_LED  -> J2  = YELLOW
	LED0 / pin 32  -> R107 4.7k to DVCC3V3_PHY   = CFG_EXT strap, no LED

LED0 is therefore not an indicator here at all; it is the strap that puts the
RGMII I/O pad on external power (DS section 7.9 Table 7-3). Both LED drivers on
this board are active high, because CFG_LDO0 (R112) and CFG_LDO1 (R110) are
strapped low (DS section 7.10, Fig. 7-5).

The register is LCR, page 0xD04 offset 0x10 (DS Table 8-2), and its bits are
given twice in the datasheet: as a per-LED grid in Table 7-4 (section 7.11) and
as a bit table with defaults in Table 8-22 (section 8.4.19). They agree:

	bit 15  LED_MODE          0 = mode A, 1 = mode B
	bit 14/13/11/10  LED2 act / link1000 / link100 / link10   defaults 1/1/0/0
	bit  9/ 8/ 6/ 5  LED1 act / link1000 / link100 / link10   defaults 1/0/1/0
	bit  4/ 3/ 1/ 0  LED0 act / link1000 / link100 / link10   defaults 1/0/0/1
	bits 2, 7, 12    reserved, RO

Those defaults spell 0x6251, which is exactly what an MDIO read of the register
returned on the board before `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` ran, and they decode to Table 7-7's "LED0 =
10M link+act, LED1 = 100M link+act, LED2 = 1000M link+act". Datasheet and
silicon agree on this register to the bit, which is what makes the rest safe.

`net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` ORed LED1_LINK_1000 and LED1_LINK_10 in, giving 0x6371: green then reads
"Link 10/100/1000 + Active 10/100/1000" and yellow keeps "Link 1000 + Active
1000". On a gigabit link both are lit and both blink on traffic. Correct as far
as it goes -- green is no longer dark -- but the two LEDs became redundant.

Split them the conventional way: green = link, steady, at any speed; yellow =
activity. That needs an activity-only code, and only LED mode B has one: in
mode A the code link=0,0,0/act=1 is listed as "N/A" (Table 7-5), while in mode
B the same code is "Active 10/100/1000Mbps" (Table 7-6). So set bit 15 and
program

	LED1 (green)  link10=1 link100=1 link1000=1 act=0
	              -> Table 7-6 "Link 10/100/1000Mbps"
	LED2 (yellow) link10=0 link100=0 link1000=0 act=1
	              -> Table 7-6 "Active 10/100/1000Mbps"

which turns the read-modify-write from an OR into a masked one, since bits now
have to be cleared as well as set. LED0's four bits are read and written back
untouched: no LED hangs on that pin, and CFG_EXT is sampled at reset only, so
mode B reinterpreting them is unobservable on this board. On the A7S the
sequence is therefore 0x6251 -> 0xc171.

The defines are renamed and completed from Table 8-22 rather than kept as the
two ad-hoc ones `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver` needed, so the next person editing this does not have to
re-derive the bit positions.

This is cosmetic. It changes no timing, no link parameter and no MAC register.

Anchors:
  DS = MAE0621A Gigabit Ethernet Transceiver datasheet v1.1 (2024-08),
       Table 7-3 (7.9), Fig. 7-5 (7.10), Tables 7-4/7-5/7-6/7-7 (7.11),
       Table 8-2 (8.2), Table 8-22 (8.4.19).
  SCH = board schematic RS504 rev V1.10 page 15 (U6 LED pins, R93/R101/R103,
       R107/R110/R112).
  SI  = LCR read 0x6251 before `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver`'s write, quoted in `net-phy-add-maxio-mae0621a-driver`.

Which colour gets which job is a choice, not a fact: the schematic fixes green
to LED1 and yellow to LED2, and green-for-link is the usual RJ45 convention.
Nothing in the datasheet or the schematic prescribes it.

Verified: applies clean on the full series over pristine 6.18.36, and
drivers/net/phy/maxio.o builds with W=1 KCFLAGS=-Werror. NOT verified on
hardware -- an LED is only provable by looking at it.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/maxio.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/maxio.c
@@ -22,8 +22,26 @@
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_INSR				0x1d
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_PAGE			0xd04
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR			0x10
-#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LED1_LINK_1000		BIT(8)
-#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LED1_LINK_10		BIT(5)
+/* LCR bit assignment: DS v1.1 Table 8-22 (section 8.4.19), cross-read with the
+ * per-LED bit table 7-4 (section 7.11). Bits 2, 7 and 12 are reserved/RO.
+ */
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED_MODE_B		BIT(15)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_ACT		BIT(14)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_LINK_1000	BIT(13)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_LINK_100	BIT(11)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_LINK_10		BIT(10)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_ACT		BIT(9)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_1000	BIT(8)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_100	BIT(6)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_10		BIT(5)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_MASK		(MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_ACT | \
+						 MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_1000 | \
+						 MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_100 | \
+						 MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_10)
+#define MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_MASK		(MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_ACT | \
+						 MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_LINK_1000 | \
+						 MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_LINK_100 | \
+						 MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_LINK_10)
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_CLK_MODE_REG      0x02
 #define MAXIO_MAE0621A_WORK_STATUS_REG   0x1d
 
@@ -139,16 +157,42 @@
 	phy_write(phydev, MAXIO_PAGE_SELECT, 0x0);
 
 	/*
-	 * LED: power-on default LCR (page 0xd04 reg 0x10) maps the green LED
-	 * (LED1 per board wiring) to 100Mbps-link only, so it is dark on a
-	 * gigabit link. Make LED1 track link at 1000/100/10Mbps + activity
-	 * (verified by live MDIO read: 0x6251 -> 0x6371).
+	 * LEDs. The Cubie A7S wires two of the PHY's three LED pins to the RJ45
+	 * (SCH rev V1.10 p15): LED1 (pin 33) drives the green LED through R93,
+	 * LED2 (pin 34) the yellow one through R101/R103. LED0 (pin 32) drives
+	 * no LED -- it is the CFG_EXT strap, pulled up by R107.
+	 *
+	 * The LCR power-on default is 0x6251 (DS Table 8-22 defaults, confirmed
+	 * by MDIO readback on this board) = LED0 10M, LED1 100M, LED2 1000M,
+	 * each link + activity (DS Table 7-7). On a gigabit link that leaves
+	 * green dark and only yellow alive.
+	 *
+	 * Give the two LEDs the conventional split instead: green = link,
+	 * steady, at any speed; yellow = activity. An activity-only code exists
+	 * only in LED mode B (LCR bit 15 = 1, DS Table 7-6), so select it. Per
+	 * mode B, LED1 = link{10,100,1000} + act 0 is "Link 10/100/1000Mbps" and
+	 * LED2 = link 0 + act 1 is "Active 10/100/1000Mbps".
+	 *
+	 * LED0's four bits are left exactly as read. That pin carries no LED,
+	 * and CFG_EXT is sampled only at reset (DS section 7.9), so mode B
+	 * reinterpreting those bits cannot be observed on this board.
+	 *
+	 * Expected result on this board: 0x6251 -> 0xc171.
 	 */
 	ret = maxio_read_paged(phydev, MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_PAGE, MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR);
-	if (ret >= 0)
-		maxio_write_paged(phydev, MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_PAGE, MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR,
-				  ret | MAXIO_MAE0621A_LED1_LINK_1000 |
-				  MAXIO_MAE0621A_LED1_LINK_10);
+	if (ret >= 0) {
+		u16 lcr = ret & ~(MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_MASK |
+				  MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_MASK);
+
+		lcr |= MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED_MODE_B;
+		lcr |= MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_10 |
+		       MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_100 |
+		       MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED1_LINK_1000;
+		lcr |= MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_LED2_ACT;
+
+		maxio_write_paged(phydev, MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR_PAGE,
+				  MAXIO_MAE0621A_LCR, lcr);
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
